{
  "id": "Compound::DB00741 - Disease::DOID:7148",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) through a mechanism involving its role in causing conditions that are treated by other corticosteroids. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone may contribute to RA through a chain of events that begins with its ability to cause Adrenocortical Insufficiency Acute, Proximal Myopathy, or Pseudotumor, all of which are conditions that are treated by Betamethasone or Dexamethasone. These corticosteroids, in turn, are known to have therapeutic effects on RA, indicating a potential pathway where Hydrocortisone's involvement in these conditions may indirectly influence RA pathology. The shared lowest common ancestor, Hydrocortisone and the corticosteroids, underscores a broader ontological relationship that links corticosteroids to RA through their effects on various physiological processes. This integrated evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone may contribute to RA through a mechanism that involves the cascade of effects triggered by corticosteroids, highlighting a potential role in the development of RA through the modulation of inflammatory or metabolic pathways.",
